// The sections of the English guide about markup and components; guide.mango lists them all.

import { example } from "../example.mango"

export func markup() Node {
    return <>
        <p>
            Markup is an ordinary expression: <code>&lt;a href="/"&gt;</code> creates a real
            {" "}<code>HTMLAnchorElement</code>, with no virtual DOM. The syntax is that of JSX, but
            attribute names are the HTML ones: <code>class</code>, not <code>className</code>.
            Types are checked as well: <code>&lt;input&gt;</code> is an
            {" "}<code>HTMLInputElement</code>, and <code>{"checked={1}"}</code> is an error.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>on*</code> attributes hold code that runs on the event:
            {" "}<code>{"onClick={save()}"}</code> calls <code>save</code> on a click, not when the
            element is created. The variable <code>event</code> is available inside.
        </p>
        {example("markup")}
        <p>
            Inside <code>{"{...}"}</code> you can write ordinary <code>if</code>, <code>for</code>
            and <code>switch</code>: the elements in their blocks become the content.
        </p>
        {example("markup-flow")}
    </>
}

export func components() Node {
    return <>
        <p>
            A component is declared with <code>comp</code> and used as a tag with a capital letter.
            Its parameters are its properties: they are checked like the arguments of a function,
            they can have default values, and <code>children Content</code> gets the markup between
            the tags. An object with the properties can be passed whole:
            {" "}<code>{"<Product {...product} />"}</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Components do not exist in JS: every use is replaced by the component's code. The
            {" "}<b>JavaScript</b> tab shows only the elements being created.
        </p>
        {example("components")}
        <p>
            A component can use itself, for a tree or nested comments. Such a component cannot be
            inlined, so it becomes a function, and every call of it has its own state. The recursion
            must end: the compiler accepts a use inside <code>{"{if ...}"}</code> or
            {" "}<code>{"{for ...}"}</code> or after an early <code>return</code>, but not an
            unconditional one. An early <code>return</code> picks the markup once, when the
            component is created.
        </p>
        {example("tree")}
        <p>
            A component exported with <code>export comp</code> is imported by its name, as in
            {" "}<code>{'import { Card } from "./card.mango"'}</code>, and used as a tag. Its module
            compiles it to a function too, which the other modules call: its code stays in its own
            module.
        </p>
    </>
}

export func reactivity() Node {
    return <>
        <p>
            <code>state</code> declares a variable that the markup depends on. The compiler knows
            which parts of the markup read it and where it changes, and after each change it updates
            exactly those parts. There are no subscriptions and no DOM diffing, as the
            {" "}<b>JavaScript</b> tab shows.
        </p>
        {example("counter")}
        <p>
            <code>bind:value</code> and <code>bind:checked</code> bind an input to state both ways.
        </p>
        {example("bind")}
        <p>
            <code>{"{if ...}"}</code> and <code>{"{for ...}"}</code> update too. A branch is created
            again only when the condition changes, and the blocks of a list are found by the array
            item itself and moved, not created again.
        </p>
        {example("todos")}
    </>
}

export func mounting() Node {
    return <>
        <p>
            <code>{"mount() { ... }"}</code> in a component's body is code that needs the markup on
            the page: starting a timer, listening to window events, setting the focus, measuring an
            element, loading data.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>mount</code> runs <b>for every instance</b> of the component: with two
            {" "}<code>{"<Fruit />"}</code> in a list, it runs twice. And it runs <b>only when the
            markup is in the document</b>. That is checked after all the synchronous code that
            created the component, in a microtask: by then <code>document.body.append(...)</code>
            or an <code>{"{if}"}</code> branch has usually inserted the markup, and the browser has
            not drawn a frame yet, so <code>focus()</code> and measuring sizes work. Markup created
            earlier and inserted later, say in a timer, mounts when it is inserted.
        </p>
        {example("mount")}
        <p>
            If <code>mount</code> returns a function, it runs when <code>{"{if}"}</code>,
            {" "}<code>{"{switch}"}</code>, <code>{"{for}"}</code> or a <code>?:</code> branch in
            markup removes the component: that is where you stop what <code>mount</code> started.
            Press "Run", then hide the clock: the timer stops.
        </p>
        {example("mount-cleanup")}
        <ul>
            <li>
                The body of <code>mount</code> is a function that runs later, like an event
                handler: changes of <code>state</code> in it update the markup, and
                {" "}<code>return</code> returns from it, not from the component.
            </li>
            <li>
                It can return a function without parameters, <code>null</code> when there is
                nothing to stop, or nothing.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>mount</code> is written at the top level of a component's body; there can be
                several, and they run in order.
            </li>
            <li>
                If <code>{"{if}"}</code>, <code>{"{switch}"}</code> or <code>{"{for}"}</code> removes
                the markup before it gets into the document, <code>mount</code> does not run.
            </li>
            <li>
                The compiler does not track markup that the program removes by itself
                (<code>element.remove()</code>): its cleanup does not run.
            </li>
            <li>
                To do something once for a whole list, write <code>mount</code> in the component
                that creates the list. Code needed once when the program starts goes simply after
                {" "}<code>document.body.append</code>.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </>
}

export func webComponents() Node {
    return <>
        <p>
            <code>@html-tag</code> before <code>comp</code> makes a component a web component as
            well: a custom element that works in plain HTML, in other frameworks and from JS, like
            {" "}<code>{"<video>"}</code>. The tag is the name of the component in kebab case,
            {" "}<code>@html-tag comp mainPage()</code> gives <code>{"<main-page>"}</code>, or the
            one in the decorator: <code>@html-tag("app-page") comp MainPage()</code>. HTML wants a
            hyphen in the name of a custom element, so <code>@html-tag comp Page()</code> is a
            compile error.
        </p>
        {example("html-tag")}
        <ul>
            <li>
                The markup goes into the element's <b>shadow root</b> when the element gets into
                the document. <code>{"{children}"}</code> is a <code>{"<slot>"}</code>: what is
                written between the tags shows up there.
            </li>
            <li>
                Every property is a <b>JS property</b> of the element, and setting it updates the
                markup. Strings, numbers and bools also have <b>attributes</b>, converted by the
                type of the property: a string as it is, a number through <code>Number()</code>,
                a bool is true when the attribute is there. <code>itemCount</code> is the
                attribute <code>item-count</code>. Without the attribute the property has its
                default value.
            </li>
            <li>
                Arrays, objects and functions are set only as JS properties:
                {" "}<code>list.items = [...]</code>. An element written in HTML has none of them
                yet, so such properties need a default value or a nullable type.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>mount</code> runs when the element is in the document, and the function it
                returns runs when the element is removed. Moving the element keeps its markup and
                state.
            </li>
            <li>
                The element is defined with <code>customElements.define</code> at the end of the
                module, when the names it uses have their values. Properties set before that are
                kept.
            </li>
            <li>
                Properties cannot be named like those of <code>HTMLElement</code> that the element
                needs: <code>id</code>, <code>style</code>, <code>hidden</code>...
            </li>
            <li>
                The playground on this page defines the elements again on every run, so their tags
                get a number: <code>{"<fruit-badge-3>"}</code>.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </>
}

export func decorators() Node {
    return <>
        <p>
            A decorator adds logic to components: state, functions and <code>mount()</code>. It is
            declared with <code>dec</code> and applied with <code>@name(arguments)</code> before
            {" "}<code>comp</code>. Like a component, it exists only at compile time: its code is
            inlined into every component it is applied to, and each of them gets its own state.
        </p>
        {example("decorators")}
        <ul>
            <li>
                The component sees only what is <b>public</b>: it reads <code>public state</code>
                {" "}and <code>public const</code> with <code>get(@ticker.ticks)</code> and calls a
                {" "}<code>public func</code> as <code>@ticker.toggle()</code>. In markup,
                {" "}<code>get</code> updates like the component's own state. Other names of the
                decorator are its own and cannot clash with the component's.
            </li>
            <li>
                Outside the decorator its members are <b>read-only</b>:
                {" "}<code>get(@ticker.ticks) = 0</code> is an error, changes go through its public
                functions. There is no <code>public let</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                The arguments are positional, as in a call, and the parameters may have default
                values: <code>@ticker</code> ticks every second. The arguments see the properties
                of the component, <code>@ticker(label.length * 100)</code>, and are computed again
                when they change.
            </li>
            <li>
                With several decorators, <code>@a @b comp X</code>, their bodies run from top to
                bottom before the component's, and <code>mount()</code> runs from the inside out:
                the component, <code>b</code>, <code>a</code>. <code>@html-tag</code> goes first.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            A decorator can also <b>wrap</b> what the component returns: its body ends with
            {" "}<code>{"return (content Element) => ..."}</code>, which gets the component's markup
            and gives other markup instead. A decorator that <code>needs</code> another one sees its
            public members.
        </p>
        {example("decorator-wrappers")}
        <ul>
            <li>
                The type of the wrapper's parameter is written: it limits where the decorator can
                be applied, so <code>{"(form HTMLFormElement) => ..."}</code> fits only components
                that return a form. <code>{"<Product />"}</code> has the type of what the outermost
                wrapper returns.
            </li>
            <li>
                The wrapper runs once, when the component is created. Its markup reads the
                decorator's state and updates like the component's own. The wrappers apply from
                the bottom decorator up.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>dec quantityBadge() needs quantity</code> may use
                {" "}<code>get(@quantity.count)</code> even in its body, so <code>@quantity</code>
                {" "}must be applied above it.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>export dec</code> exports a decorator, and another module imports it by its
                name: <code>{'import { quantity } from "./quantity.mango"'}</code>. Its code is
                inlined there, so it cannot assign the variables of its own module, and the
                decorators it needs are exported too.
            </li>
            <li>Not yet: combining decorators.</li>
        </ul>
    </>
}
